<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasimov</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gorodets Meshchersky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kasimov is a town on the Oka river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Originally called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a> Meshchersky, it was granted in 1452 by Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II of Moscow</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> prince Qasim, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a>, and became the seat of the Qasim Khanate, a Muscovite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> ruled by Chinggisid princes until 1681. A minaret and the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-ali-khan">Shah Ali Khan</a> survive from that era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasimov.jpg" alt="Kasimov" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Voropz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasimov_view.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first inhabitants of the area were a Finnic tribe called the Meshchyora, later assimilated by Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. The Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> ruler Yuri Dolgorukiy founded the town in 1152 as Grodets, then Gorodets Meshchyorsky. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed it in 1376, and it was soon rebuilt as Novy Nizovoy. After the Battle of Suzdal in 1445, in which Grand Duke Vasily II was taken prisoner, the Meshchyora lands were given to Oluğ Möxämmäd, Khan of the Kazan Khanate, as a ransom for the sovereign's life.</p><p>In 1452 Vasily II, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, gave the town to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> prince Qasim, who served as tribute inspector of the Great Horde and then entered Russian service. By other accounts Qasim and his brother Yosif fled from Kazan after losing a contest for the throne to their brother Mäxmüd. After 1471 the town was known as Qasim city, and it remained the capital of the Qasim Khanate until the khanate was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1681.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> settled in Kasimov in the 15th century and are now known as Qasim Tatars. They speak the Mishar dialect, mixed with the Middle Tatar dialect of the Tatar language. In the 19th century the town became known for its waiters, who staffed many of the hotels of St Petersburg. In the 17th century it was divided in three: the Old Town and the Tatar Town, ruled by the khan and Tatar noblemen, the Yamskoy Town of Russian commoners, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and the Marfin Town, ruled by the Kasimov voivodes.</p><p>Historical buildings include the Khan's Mosque, with a limestone minaret dated either to 1467 or to the 1550s, the mausoleum of Shahghali Khan of 1555 and that of Afghan Moxammad Khan of 1658. Four Russian Orthodox churches also stand there: the Church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a>, St Nicholas Church and Trinity Church of the 17th century, and the Cathedral of the Ascension of the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kasimov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1452 Vasily II of Moscow granted the town of <b>Gorodets Meshchersky</b> on the Oka, later renamed Kasimov, to Qasim, a son of Ulugh Muhammad, founder of the Khanate of Kazan, who had entered Moscow's service.</blockquote><a href="/state/qasim-khanate">Qasim Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
